<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Upper Bavaria</h1><p class="st-meta">1255 CE – 1340 CE · capital Munich</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Wittelsbach duchy ruled from Munich, created in the first partition of Bavaria in 1255 and home of Emperor Louis IV the Bavarian.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1255 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-ii-duke-of-bavaria">Otto II</a>'s sons divided the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria</a>, the first of many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbach</a> partitions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-duke-of-bavaria">Louis II the Strict</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-bavaria">Upper Bavaria</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> as residence, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-region">Rhenish Palatinate</a>, while his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-xiii-duke-of-bavaria">Henry XIII</a> took the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-lower-bavaria">Duchy of Lower Bavaria</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-duke-of-bavaria">Louis II</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> a ducal capital and acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> lands after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conradin">Conradin's</a> execution in 1268. His sons Rudolf I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Louis IV</a> ruled jointly, then fought each other. Louis IV was elected King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 1314, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-fair">Frederick the Fair</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhldorf">Mühldorf</a> in 1322 and was crowned emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1328. By the Treaty of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-pavia">Pavia</a> in 1329 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Palatinate</a> went to Rudolf's heirs.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the Lower Bavarian line died out in 1340, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Louis IV</a> reunited Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-bavaria">Lower Bavaria</a>. After his death in 1347 his sons divided the lands again, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> remained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbach</a> capital until 1918.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-upper-bavaria.jpg" alt="Duchy of Upper Bavaria" width="320" height="316"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_map_RB_Oberbayern_in_Bavaria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1255 CE – 1340 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Munich</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Louis II the Strict, Rudolf I of the Palatinate, Louis IV the Bavarian</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bavarian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Oberbayern</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1298&amp;focus=duchy-of-upper-bavaria" aria-label="Duchy of Upper Bavaria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4949 493 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Upper Bavaria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5269 749L5279 723"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5279 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5269 749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Munich. <a href="/atlas?year=1298&amp;focus=duchy-of-upper-bavaria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
